{{Life in Balisca}}

'''Rural society''' in [[Balisca]], often referred to as '''''El Campo''''' ({{Literal translation|the countryside}}), consists of approximately 60% of Balisca's land area. An estimated 14 million people, or less than one-in-ten residents (approximately 9% of the total Baliscan population in the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]]) live in rural Balisca. Definitions vary from different parts of society as to what constitutes these areas. 

Rural areas tend to be less built up and closer to their natural state. Contrasting with rural populations of typical {{W|advanced economy|advanced economies}}, Balisca has a comparatively young rural population that has been consistently becoming younger thanks to {{W|free migration}} policies, relocation of {{W|NEET}} individuals from urban areas, accelerating {{W|automation}} which has encouraged a {{W|leisure society}} and widespread cultural {{W|Critique of work|re-evaluation of the concept of work}}.

== [[W:Counterurbanization|Counterurbanization]] and environmentalism ==
The resurgence of popular folk practices and beliefs after 1970s and 80s was largely due to the lack of economic advances in most of rural Balisca and increasing economic instability. Following the [[October 76]] events, as many as 3 million people, mostly young people sporting [[Desconexión|''desconexión'']] fashions of dress, behavior and ideologies left the cities and moved to [[W:Intentional community|rural communes]]. More broadly, this period encompassed the rise of Baliscan [[W:House music|house]] and [[W:Psychedelic music|psychedelic]] music, {{W|hallucinogenic drugs}}, renewed [[Politics of Balisca|anti-statism]], and the {{W|free love|free-love}} scene throughout the Confederation.

Many of these individuals suspicious of the government, {{W|anti-consumerism|rejected consumerist values}}, and generally rejected [[Baliscan accelerationism|Accelerationist]] growth and opposed {{W|urban sprawl}} which was having detrimental effects on the Baliscan environment. A significant number were interested in politics, leading to the establishment of [[Ecological Action]] and the first [[Zone to Defend|Zones to Defend]]; others were concerned more with art (music, painting, poetry in particular) or spiritual and meditative practices, resulting in surges in {{W|deep ecology|deep ecological views}}, {{W|ecospirituality}}, {{W|gaianism}} and other {{W|nature religion}}s.

Progressively, this [[W:Counterurbanization|counter-urban]] vision has changed its goal, turning from simple opposition to urban expansion, to the promotion of {{W|degrowth}}, {{W|compact cities}}, {{W|restoration ecology}} and {{W|rewilding}} to halt and eventually reverse {{W|degradation of the environment}}. In Balisca, the politics of the city rest on a libertine and utopian vision of urbanism, and on an enchantment with the city center. For a long time, rural Baliscan society has remained preoccupied with a sentiment of hostility to the city stemming from their encroachment on the environment and the altering of the natural ecosystem. The country and the rural society are perceived as holding and conserving "authentic" [[Baliscan values]]—notably with regard to the environment— including {{W|Communalism (Bookchin)|communalism}}, opposition to authority, connection with land, and sense of responsibility to the {{W|common good}}.

To prevent these ills and {{W|counterurbanization}}, rural Balisca embraced {{W|Free public transport|free and cooperative transit}} and increased {{W|population density}} to prevent {{W|sprawl}} and {{W|car dependency}}, and {{W|active design|active}} and {{W|Healthy city|healthy communities}} to ensure high {{W|quality of life}} and sharing of {{W|economic resources}}. Additionally, it is largely responsible for the lack of lower density {{W|suburb|suburban development}} around Baliscan cities throughout the country. Paradoxically, this influential environmentally-oriented outlook then gave rise to widespread {{W|rural–urban fringe}} and {{W|wildland–urban interface}} across the country.

